{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Final Project and Professional Vision","title":"Synthesis Across Modules","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting synthesis across modules to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"01","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/08"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Synthesis Across Modules\n\n![Synthesis Across Modules textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-01-synthesis-across-modules.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAcross the first five modules of CAT 531 you analyzed pedagogy, equity, classroom logistics, and data ethics as if they were separate lenses you could pick up one at a time. Real teaching decisions never arrive that cleanly. When a colleague proposes an AI essay-feedback tool in a department meeting, the pedagogical promise (faster revision cycles), the equity risk (uneven home access), and the data boundary (whether student writing is used for training) all press on the same choice at the same moment, and they frequently pull in opposite directions. Synthesis is the skill of holding those competing claims together and producing a single decision you can defend, not a list of considerations you noticed.\n\nThe artifact for this section, the Design Tension Studio map, exists to make that synthesis visible. A tension map does not ask you to resolve a conflict by pretending it disappeared; it asks you to name which value you let win, which you constrained, and what you gave up. That record is the difference between a teacher who says \"I weighed the options\" and one who can show, on paper, the weighing itself.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA seventh-grade team is deciding whether to adopt an adaptive reading platform. The literacy coach loves the individualized pacing (a Module 2 pedagogy argument). The special-education teacher worries the platform's reading-level labels will follow students into other classes as a fixed identity (a Module 3 equity-and-labeling concern). The technology coordinator notes the vendor's contract permits resale of usage analytics (a Module 5 datafication boundary). Each colleague is right within their own lens. The question synthesis forces is not \"who is correct\" but \"what decision honors the strongest claim from each lens at once, and which claim are we knowingly subordinating.\"\n\nA teacher who can answer that, naming the subordinated value out loud, has done synthesis. A teacher who simply adopts because the pacing is good, or refuses because the contract is bad, has only applied one lens and called it a decision.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nSynthesis across modules is the disciplined act of resolving a multi-lens conflict into one accountable choice. It has three properties that distinguish it from a checklist:\n\n- **It surfaces a genuine tension, not a list.** A checklist says \"consider pedagogy, equity, and data.\" A synthesis names the specific place where pedagogy and equity collide for this tool, in this class, this term. If your map could describe any tool, it has not located a tension yet.\n- **It assigns priority, not equality.** Treating every value as equally weighted is a refusal to decide. Synthesis states which value governs when two cannot both be fully satisfied, and gives the reason that ranking holds here (for example, \"access governs efficiency because a revision the device-less student cannot reach is not a revision\").\n- **It records the cost.** Every real decision sacrifices something. A defensible map names what the chosen path gives up so a colleague can judge whether the trade was worth it.\n\nThe three course anchors do real work only when each one can change the decision:\n\n| Anchor | What it forces you to ask | A change it can produce |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TPACK | Does the tool serve a content-and-pedagogy demand, or just a generic \"engagement\" goal? | Drop a flashy tool whose AI feature does not touch the actual learning target. |\n| Critical edtech studies | Who carries new burden or risk that the marketing hides? | Add a fallback for students the default workflow would exclude. |\n| AI and education datafication | What happens to student data, and who profits from it? | Require an in-class, no-training-data configuration before adoption. |\n\nIf an anchor leaves your decision identical, you cited it; you did not use it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A map reads: 'We will weigh pacing benefits, access risks, and data terms equally and use the platform thoughtfully.' Which synthesis property is this missing?\" explanation=\"Treating every value as equally weighted is a refusal to decide. Synthesis must assign priority: state which value governs when two cannot both be fully satisfied, and give the reason that ranking holds here.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It surfaces a genuine tension rather than a list\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It assigns priority rather than equality\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It records the cost of the chosen path\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It cites all three course anchors\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft often hides the real decision behind a vague improvement claim:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nNotice the shift: the artifact is no longer just polished. The revision points to **critical edtech studies**, names the evidence source, and preserves the reason a suggestion was accepted or rejected.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Synthesis Decision\n\nFollow one teacher building a tension map for an AI writing-feedback tool across the module lenses.\n\n1. **Scenario.** A 9th-grade teacher pilots an AI tool that gives instant feedback on student essays. Six of 28 students have no reliable internet at home; the tool's free tier caps revisions at three per week.\n2. **Surface the conflict (before).** First draft of the map: \"AI feedback helps students revise faster, so I will adopt it.\" This is a single-lens decision (pedagogy only) with no named tension.\n3. **Apply each module lens.**\n   - Pedagogy (Module 2): faster revision cycles can raise draft quality.\n   - Equity (Module 3): the 6 students without home access get fewer revision cycles, widening a gap.\n   - Ethics and datafication (Module 5): essays are student data; the free tier's terms allow training on submissions.\n4. **Negotiate the collision (after).** Revised decision: \"Adopt the tool for in-class use only, on school devices, with a no-training data setting, and a printed-feedback fallback for the 6 students. The equity lens overrides the pure-efficiency option.\"\n5. **Record the rejected suggestion.** BigAL proposed assigning the tool as homework to save class time. Rejected: it reintroduces the home-access gap the equity lens flagged.\n\nThe before says what is convenient; the after shows a negotiated judgment a colleague can inspect, including the value that was allowed to win and why.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The adaptive-reading-platform adoption meeting\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your seventh-grade team is split: the literacy coach wants the platform for individualized pacing, the special-education teacher fears its reading-level labels will follow students, and the tech coordinator notes the contract permits reselling usage analytics. As lead, what is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt it: the pacing gains are too good to pass up\",\"next\":\"adopt\",\"feedback\":\"You applied one lens (pedagogy) and called it a decision. Synthesis requires naming the collision, not picking the loudest benefit.\"},{\"label\":\"Name the specific collision: pacing benefit versus a labeling-and-data cost for these students this term\",\"next\":\"name\",\"feedback\":\"This locates a real tension rather than listing concerns, the first property of synthesis.\"},{\"label\":\"Table it indefinitely so no value is harmed\",\"next\":\"table\",\"feedback\":\"Refusing to decide is not synthesis; it leaves the loudest default to win by inertia.\"}]},\"adopt\":{\"prompt\":\"Two weeks in, a parent asks why their child was placed in a remedial group based on the platform label. You have no record of weighing that risk. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reconstruct the reasoning after the fact\",\"next\":\"posthoc\",\"feedback\":\"A justification written after the decision is not a reasoning trace; the collision was never actually negotiated.\"},{\"label\":\"Restart with a tension map that names the governing value\",\"next\":\"name\",\"feedback\":\"Returning to the synthesis discipline is correct, though the cost of skipping it has already landed on a student.\"}]},\"table\":{\"prompt\":\"The coach adopts the platform in her own room anyway. Tabling produced a decision you did not make. What does this teach about indecision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Indecision is itself a choice that lets a default value win\",\"next\":\"name\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Synthesis lets a value win deliberately rather than by drift.\"}]},\"name\":{\"prompt\":\"You have named the collision: individualized pacing (a learning gain) versus persistent labeling plus analytics resale (an equity-and-data cost). Which value should govern, and on what reason?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Equity-and-data governs, because a label that follows a child into other classes is a harm the pacing gain cannot offset here\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Strong: you assigned priority with a reason specific to this class and term, and you can now record the cost.\"},{\"label\":\"Pacing governs, because all values matter equally so efficiency breaks the tie\",\"next\":\"equal\",\"feedback\":\"Equal weighting is a refusal to rank. If everything matters equally, you have not decided which one governs.\"}]},\"equal\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: when two values cannot both be fully satisfied, what does synthesis demand?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"State which value governs and why the ranking holds here\",\"next\":\"win\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, priority with a local reason is the move.\"}]},\"win\":{\"prompt\":\"You decide equity-and-data governs. What must your map record to be defensible?\",\"feedback\":\"A defensible map names the collision, states the governing value with its reason, and records the cost: here, the team gives up the individualized pacing gain, a trade you judged worth it because a persistent label is harder to undo than slower pacing. That recorded cost is what makes the map honest rather than promotional.\",\"choices\":[]},\"posthoc\":{\"prompt\":\"The after-the-fact rationale reads tidy but a colleague cannot see the collision being negotiated. What is the lesson?\",\"feedback\":\"Write the map as a reasoning trace before deciding, so a colleague sees the value being weighed, not a defense of a choice that was never in doubt.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen two course lenses collide on the same tool, name the binding constraint before you reach for the affordance. A platform feature is only worth discussing once you have established that the feature does not violate the value you have decided governs the case.\n\nUse the following check before you call a tension map finished:\n\n1. **Tension check:** Have I named a specific collision between two values for this exact tool and class, or only listed concerns?\n2. **Priority check:** Have I stated which value governs and given the reason that ranking holds here?\n3. **Cost check:** Have I named what the chosen path sacrifices and what suggestion I rejected to protect the governing value?\n\nIf any answer is missing, the map is incomplete as evidence of synthesis even if it looks finished.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why 'we will use the tool a little, for some students, sometimes' fails as synthesis even though it touches every value. What would a genuine priority statement say instead?\" answer=\"That sentence dilutes every value until none is honored and none is clearly subordinated, so the decision becomes unfalsifiable: no colleague could ever say it ranked the values wrongly because it ranked nothing. Synthesis instead lets one value win deliberately and records the cost. A genuine priority statement names the governing value and its local reason, for example: 'Equity of access governs over revision efficiency for this class because six students cannot reach a home-based revision, so a benefit they cannot use is not a benefit; the cost is fewer total revisions for the class, which I accept because the floor matters more than the ceiling here.'\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most frequent error is treating synthesis as compromise, splitting the difference so no value is fully honored and none is clearly subordinated. A map that says \"we will use the tool a little, for some students, sometimes\" reads as indecision dressed up as balance. Synthesis lets a value win; it does not dilute every value until the decision is unfalsifiable.\n\nA second misreading is writing the map after the decision is already made, as a justification rather than a reasoning trace. A colleague should be able to see the collision being negotiated, not a tidy rationale for a choice that was never actually in doubt.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply this to a real adoption meeting. A middle-school team is choosing between two AI math tutors. Tutor A adapts faster but stores keystroke-level data the vendor may license; Tutor B is slower to adapt but stores nothing beyond scores. Begin by naming the tension precisely: adaptivity (a learning gain) versus data exposure (a student-protection cost), where the two tools force a choice rather than letting you have both.\n\nThen let an anchor change the decision. Datafication analysis asks who benefits from the keystroke data; the answer is the vendor, not the students, and that reframes Tutor A's speed as a cost passed to families. TPACK then asks whether the extra adaptivity even serves the unit's target; if the unit needs procedural fluency that Tutor B handles adequately, the marginal adaptivity buys little. The synthesis names Tutor B as the choice, states that data protection governs because the adaptivity gain is small for this target, and records the cost (students wait slightly longer for the tutor to adjust). That last sentence, the named cost, is what makes the map honest rather than promotional.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left write each value the case raises; on the right write what would have to be true for that value to govern the decision. The point is to practice ranking rather than listing. By the end of CAT 531 06.01 you should be able to take any new tool proposal and immediately ask \"which two of my values does this pit against each other, and which one should win here.\"\n\nA strong reader of synthesis can answer four questions:\n\n1. Where exactly do two course values collide for this specific tool and class?\n2. Which value should govern, and why does that ranking hold in this case rather than always?\n3. What does the governing choice cost, and is the trade defensible?\n4. What would change in the ranking if the class, the contract, or the tool changed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA tension map is strong when a colleague who disagrees with your decision can still follow your reasoning and locate the exact point where they would have ranked the values differently. Polish (clean layout, tidy arrows) is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are the named collision, the stated priority with its reason, and the recorded cost. If any of those is missing, mark the gap with an annotation and ask BigAL for a targeted comparison of two rankings rather than a general explanation of \"balancing values.\"\n\nA useful closing line for the map would read: \"I let equity of access govern over revision efficiency because a benefit students cannot reach is not a benefit; the cost is fewer total revisions for the class, which I judged acceptable because the floor matters more than the ceiling here.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same map to a wealthy district where every student has a personal device and reliable home internet. The pedagogy-versus-equity collision that governed the original decision largely dissolves; the home-access gap that made efficiency dangerous no longer exists. But a new tension surfaces: with universal access, the datafication lens may now dominate, because the very ubiquity of the tool means more student data flowing to the vendor across more of the day. If you understand synthesis, your map changes its governing value when the context changes. If you only memorized \"equity wins,\" you will defend the wrong value in the new setting.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-01-synthesis-across-modules-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 06.01. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","stem":"A teacher's Design Tension Studio map names the value conflict as \"personalized AI feedback (efficiency) versus equal access for students without reliable home internet (equity).\" Which version best shows synthesis across the CAT 531 modules rather than a single-module answer?","options":["It cites the most modules by listing TPACK, critical edtech studies, and datafication in the introduction.","It connects the pedagogy module (why the feedback is needed), the equity module (who is excluded), and the ethics module (the consent boundary) into one negotiated decision the teacher will defend.","It picks whichever module the teacher found easiest and applies only that lens.","It resolves the tension by choosing the option that needs the least classroom change."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Synthesis means letting multiple module lenses jointly shape one defensible decision, not name-dropping modules or collapsing to a single comfortable lens.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_listing_is_synthesis","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision","cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","stem":"On the tension map, a reviewer leaves the note: \"You named the equity risk in Module 3 and the consent rule in Module 5, but the map never says which one wins when they collide.\" What revision does this note call for?","options":["Add more module citations so both lenses are clearly present.","Make the map more visually balanced so neither side looks larger.","State the priority rule the teacher used to negotiate the conflict, and the evidence that justified putting one value ahead of the other in this class.","Remove the weaker of the two values so the map shows only one position."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Synthesis is not holding two lenses side by side; it is showing how the teacher negotiated the collision and why.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_tensions_left_unresolved","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"While building the tension map, the teacher asks BigAL to draft it. The strongest professional boundary is to use AI to:","options":["Generate the full negotiated decision so every module is covered consistently.","Surface a competing value the teacher may have missed, which the teacher then accepts or rejects against classroom evidence and records the reason.","Decide which value should win, since the model has read more cases.","Rewrite the map in more confident language so it reads as more defensible."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can widen the field of values to consider, but the teacher owns the negotiated judgment and the recorded rationale.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_ai_resolves_tension","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_6_1_synthesis_across_modules_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_synthesis_across_modules","stem":"Two students submit tension maps on the same AI-tutor scenario. Map A reaches the same final decision as the answer key; Map B reaches a different decision. Under a synthesis standard, when is Map B the stronger artifact?","options":["Never, because the answer key sets the correct negotiated decision.","Whenever Map B is longer and names more modules than Map A.","When Map B traces the value conflict, weighs each module lens, and justifies its different decision with classroom-specific evidence the key did not assume.","Only if 